---
import { getCollection, getEntry } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import BisslogDemo from '../../components/BisslogDemo.astro';
import ArticleList from '../../components/ArticleList.astro';
import { bisslogPackages } from '../../data/bisslog';
import type { Article } from '../../lib/articles';

const bisslogArticles = await getCollection('bisslog');
const theory = (
  await Promise.all([
    getEntry('posts', '2025-02-14-the-myth-of-lightweight-frameworks'),
    getEntry('posts', '2025-02-14-sustainable-separation-business-logic'),
  ])
).filter((entry): entry is NonNullable<typeof entry> => Boolean(entry));
const articles: Article[] = [...bisslogArticles, ...theory].sort(
  (a, b) => b.data.date.getTime() - a.data.date.getTime(),
);
---

<Base
  title="bisslog — hexagonal architecture for Python · Darwin Herrera"
  description="bisslog is an open-source Python library that keeps business logic in plain use cases and moves frameworks and infrastructure into adapters."
  contactTitle="Want this separation in your services?"
  contactText="Tell me on LinkedIn about the system. I answer every message."
>
  <section class="container head">
    <div class="intro">
      <img
        src="/assets/img/brand/bisslog-logo-imagotipo.png"
        width="180"
        height="59"
        alt="bisslog"
        class="logo"
      />
      <h1>The use case stays. Everything around it changes.</h1>
      <p class="lede text-2">
        bisslog is my open-source library for hexagonal architecture in Python. Business rules live in plain use cases
        with no framework code; HTTP, queues, Lambda and databases plug in as adapters. It is not a framework: the core
        has no external dependencies.
      </p>
      <div class="link-row">
        <a href="https://github.com/darwinhc/bisslog-core-py">Source code</a>
        <a href="https://pypi.org/project/bisslog/">PyPI</a>
        <a href="https://github.com/darwinhc/company-data-registry.microservice">Example service</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <BisslogDemo />
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="container split section">
      <h2 class="split-title">The problem</h2>
      <div class="split-body text-2">
        <p>
          In most services the business logic is written inside the framework: a Flask route opens the database client,
          applies the rules and formats the response. It works until the service has to run as a Lambda, read from a
          queue or move from MongoDB to PostgreSQL. Then the rules have to be rewritten, because they were never apart.
        </p>
        <figure class="diagram">
          <img
            src="/assets/img/entry-points-problem.png"
            width="551"
            height="355"
            alt="Diagram: a use case written inside Flask, with a PyMongo handler inside the use case talking to MongoDB. FastAPI and AWS Lambda are shown as other entry point technologies it cannot move to."
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container split section">
    <h2 class="split-title">The idea</h2>
    <div class="split-body text-2">
      <p>
        Each service is organised around its use cases, which say <strong>what</strong> the system does. How it is
        triggered (HTTP, EventBridge, Lambda, a CLI) and where the data lives (MongoDB, PostgreSQL, S3) are adapters
        behind ports that the use cases define. An entry point is just another adapter.
      </p>
      <figure class="diagram">
        <img
          src="/assets/img/entry-points-explanation.png"
          width="621"
          height="390"
          alt="Diagram: possible entry points (AWS Lambda, FastAPI, Flask, Django, RabbitMQ, Kafka) are mapped to a microservice whose use cases talk to ports for the database, publisher, notifier, file upload and WebSockets; adapters such as PostgreSQL, PyMongo and RabbitMQ implement those ports."
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </figure>
      <p>
        Service and use-case metadata is written in YAML, so the HTTP routes, Lambda handlers and FastAPI apps can be
        generated instead of written by hand. Because the rules are declared apart from any framework, translating a use
        case to another stack, by hand or with AI assistance, is a contained task.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container section flush" aria-labelledby="packages-title">
    <h2 id="packages-title">Packages</h2>
    <ul class="packages">
      {
        bisslogPackages.map((pkg) => (
          <li>
            <a class="mono" href={pkg.href}>
              {pkg.name}
            </a>
            <span>{pkg.text}</span>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="container section flush" aria-labelledby="reading-title">
    <h2 id="reading-title">Read more</h2>
    <div class="list">
      <ArticleList articles={articles} />
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .head {
    padding-top: 80px;
    padding-bottom: 96px;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 56px;
    align-items: center;
  }

  .intro {
    flex: 1 1 420px;
    min-width: 0;
    max-width: 540px;
  }

  .logo {
    width: 150px;
    margin-bottom: 28px;
  }

  .lede {
    margin: 24px 0 28px;
  }

  .section {
    padding-top: 104px;
    padding-bottom: 104px;
  }

  .flush {
    padding-top: 0;
  }

  .diagram {
    margin: 28px 0 0;
    padding: clamp(12px, 2vw, 24px);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: #fff;
  }

  .diagram img {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 620px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .packages {
    margin: 32px 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(min(100%, 260px), 1fr));
    column-gap: 40px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .packages li {
    padding: 16px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .packages a {
    font-size: 15px;
  }

  .packages span {
    display: block;
    font-size: 15px;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .list {
    margin-top: 32px;
  }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .head {
      padding-top: 56px;
      padding-bottom: 72px;
    }

    .section {
      padding-top: 72px;
      padding-bottom: 72px;
    }

    .flush {
      padding-top: 0;
    }
  }
</style>
